course: dictionary
lesson: 8465
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. The sentence around it tells you which one the writer means. Look at the clues, then ask: which meaning fits here?
explain: «Sally Lightfoot crabs are bright red crabs that leap from rock to rock.» Here Sally is a name, because it is the first part of the crab's name and it is written with a capital letter. Sally is also a girl's name, so the clue is the capital S and the crab.
explain: In a story, you might read that the knights «sally forth» at dawn. Forth means onward, and the sentence is about a bold start to an adventure. So sally forth means to set out boldly, often with a joke or a cheer. Try it in the sentence: "The knights set out boldly at dawn" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A sally can be a sudden attack by soldiers rushing out from a castle or town that is under siege.
fact: A sally can also be a quick, witty remark, a clever comment that makes people smile.
fact: To sally forth means to set out boldly, often jokingly, on an adventure or a journey.
fact: Sally is also a name, and «Sally Lightfoot crabs» are bright red crabs that leap from rock to rock.
exercise: In «Sally Lightfoot crabs are bright red crabs», what kind of word is Sally? | A sudden attack | A verb | *A name | A quick, witty remark
exercise: «The soldiers came out of the castle in a sally to attack the enemy.» What does sally mean here? | To set out boldly, joking | A quick, witty joke | A type of crab | *A sudden attack by soldiers rushing out
exercise: «Ella said, "Let's sally forth on an adventure!"» What does sally forth mean? | To sell things at a market | *To set out boldly, often jokingly | To hide quietly | To fall asleep
test: «Her sally at the meeting made the whole room laugh.» What is a sally here? | A girl's name | *A quick, witty remark | A sudden attack by soldiers | A type of crab
test: Which word completes this sentence? «Yesterday the knights ___ forth to explore the forest.» | sally | Sally | sallies | *sallied
exam: Why can the same word, sally, mean different things? | *Words can have several meanings, so the context shows which one the writer means | Sally is always a name, whatever the sentence says | Every word has only one meaning, so sally is a mistake | Sally only means a crab
